<article class="env-lemma" id="066F">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/066F">Lemma <span data-tag="066F">15.68.6</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring. Let $M$ be an $R$-module. Let $d \geq 0$. The following are equivalent </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$M$ has tor dimension $\leq d$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
there exists a resolution 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  0 \to F_ d \to \ldots \to F_1 \to F_0 \to M \to 0  \]
</div>
<p>
 with $F_ i$ a flat $R$-module. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> In particular an $R$-module has tor dimension $0$ if and only if it is a flat $R$-module. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume (2). Then the complex $E^\bullet $ with $E^{-i} = F_ i$ is quasi-isomorphic to $M$. Hence the Tor dimension of $M$ is at most $d$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/0654" data-tag="0654">15.68.3</a>. Conversely, assume (1). Let $P^\bullet \to M$ be a projective resolution of $M$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0653" data-tag="0653">15.68.2</a> we see that $\tau _{\geq -d}P^\bullet $ is a flat resolution of $M$ of length $d$, i.e., (2) holds. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>